      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Delays in attending to ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) are indicators or markers of quality of health services. Several records suggest gender disparity in cardiac care as a contributor to the increased mortality among women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We prospectively enrolled all consecutive STEMI patients who were transferred to our hospital from January through December 2015. The following variables were analyzed: Symptom-to-Door Time (SDT); Time to First ECG (TECG); Transfer Time to Referring Center (TTRC); and Door-to-Cath lab time (DCT)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of the 133 patients, 85 (63.9%) were male and 45 (36%) female. The mean age and body mass index (BMI) between the male and female genders were 56.3 and 60.5 years for the first and 26 and 27.7 Kg/M2 for the second. Diabetes and low school education level were more prevalent in women than men, with statistical significance: 20 (48.8%) vs 18 (26.1%) with P = 0.01 and 26 (54.2%) vs 28 (32.9%) with P = 0.04, respectively. Regarding the times evaluated (SDT, TECG, TTRC and DCT), there was no statistically significant difference in relation to gender. STEMI Killip class I was more prevalent in males: 93 (86.1%) vs 12 (63.2%) cases with P = 0.01, and thrombolysis with a tendency towards the same direction: 17 (20%) vs 4 (8.3%) and P = 0.07."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "According to our results women with STEMI had significantly higher prevalence of diabetes and low school education level, as well as a higher proportion of complicated STEMI (Killip class ≥ II)."
        }
      ]},
